infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Why SLO programs die vs survive
  desc Same failure modes, opposite outcomes — the bottleneck is the contract, not the tooling
  compares
    - label Teams that abandon SLOs
      children
        - label SLI ignores user pain
          desc Alerts fire on technical signals users never feel — on-call learns the page is noise.
        - label Target too tight
          desc Constant freezes; product pressure forces an exception and the policy loses its teeth.
        - label Policy never signed at director level
          desc Advisory SLOs with no authority — nobody acts when the budget goes negative.
        - label No quarterly review
          desc The SLO drifts from real user needs and sends the wrong signal for two years.
    - label Teams that succeed with SLOs
      children
        - label Validate the SLI against user reports
          desc Start with one journey; every page with no user complaint means the SLI is wrong.
        - label Conservative target, tighten quarterly
          desc Set it loose, confirm the baseline is stable, then ratchet the nines down.
        - label Director signs before the first freeze
          desc Authority is established before the pressure arrives — so the freeze actually holds.
        - label Quarterly review with product present
          desc Relax, tighten, or fix on real user research — the SLO stays a living target.
theme
  palette #f43f5e #14b8a6
